commit 60bd0d7880c82980f9481f8be78862e9b63c58a3
Author: prbme <34986815+prbme@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Jul 16 13:31:40 2026 -0400

    Gemma 4: aspect-preserving image resize with per-image dynamic token counts; fix multi-image requests (#405)
    
    Gemma4: aspect-preserving resize with per-image dynamic token counts
    Port the Python mlx-vlm Gemma4ImageProcessor resize to the Swift port,
    replacing the fixed-size square preprocessing.
    - Resize each image to the largest dimensions that keep the patch count
      within max_soft_tokens * pooling_kernel_size^2 with both sides divisible
      by pooling_kernel_size * patch_size, so the pooling kernel is exact and
      the pooled grid covers the image at any aspect ratio. Extreme-aspect
      inputs clamp to one pooling cell on the short side, capped at the token
      budget on the long side.
    - Expand prompt placeholders per image to numPatches / kernel^2 soft
      tokens instead of a fixed image_seq_length. The processor decodes
      max_soft_tokens / patch_size / pooling_kernel_size from a flat
      preprocessor_config.json or the nested image_processor object.
    - Carry per-image sizes through LMInput.ProcessedImage: smaller images are
      zero-padded onto the request's largest canvas, real sizes ride in frames,
      and the model slices each image back out to run the vision tower alone,
      concatenating pooled tokens in placeholder order. Multi-image requests
      (previously imageTokenCountMismatch, plus an MLX concat crash for mixed
      orientations) now work.
    - With every patch real, the padded machinery (-1 positions, attention
      mask, pooler valid-count trimming) is deleted: dense encoder attention,
      full-grid pooling. gemma4VisionPoolingKernel (#290) goes with it, since
      the resize guarantees numPatches = outputLength * kernel^2. Its
      regression tests are replaced by Gemma4ResizeTests.
    - The vestigial 'size' config entry is now ignored, matching the Python
      reference. gemma4_unified is untouched.
    Also builds patch positions on the GPU with arange + meshGrid + stacked,
    and passes patchesW into the pooler directly instead of recovering it via
    max().item() (which forced a per-image GPU sync).
    Verified against mlx-community/gemma-4-26b-a4b-it-4bit (single portrait,
    single landscape, and portrait+landscape in one message); target-size math
    cross-checked against the Python formula over 100k random sizes.

